King who made the shining heaven;
he is not proud, not overbearing [?];
and the earth, with its abundance of beauty,
equally strong, equally firm, equally mighty.

From the east the clear purple,
from the south the white, bright and wondrous,
from the north the black, stormy and harsh,
from the west the wailing dun.

The fifth zone, which burns the skin,
through whose long tract my King runs;
[?] the hosts;
two cold zones and two temperate.

In the month of July the sun is in Leo;
it is then its full struggle is waged;
the sun goes swiftly on its way [?]
into Virgo in August.

King who made every element separately,
the visible and the invisible;
except the King who shaped them,
there is no king over the earth.

There is a cross of gold on each door,
which a host of lasting light beholds;
the King fashioned them without [?];
they are thick and very high.

'Honour to Adam, I will not hide it:
since I am the elder, I will not give it;
for I was a noble angel before every lord,
so I will not bow myself beneath the younger.'

'The angels shall be beneath my foot;
I will exalt myself;
I will be lord over every throng;
there shall be no other king above me.'

Lucifer, with all his ranks,
was overthrown by his self-exaltation;
his fierce pride cast him down;
he was driven into hell.

'It is you, without concealment,
that every mother and father will serve [?];
from today for ever, a splendid triumph,
every one of us shall be at your bidding.'

'You are nobler for battle,
you were created first;
you are cleverer in every shape;
do not bow yourself beneath the younger.'

'Take my counsel without anxiety;
let us make a covenant and friendship;
listen yourself to my pure purpose,
before you go to Adam.'

'Whatever you ask [?],
you will not trouble me, O gentle, modest creature;
it is sure it will not be dark here;
I will tell you in sincerity.'

'Come, serpent, yourself to the tree,
and bring one apple from it;
if only the apple reaches me,
I will share it between me and Adam.'

Eve secretly opened
the door before the serpent;
unseen he went, he was not slow;
he ran to the one tree.

Eve took the whole apple
from the tree, a most wretched tale;
Eve ate her half, it was not pure;
she left the other half for Adam.

He promised Abram every victory
for his coming from the north-east,
for his going with him southward:
that he would have a land as his own for ever.

"Here is a meal, especially well made and wholesome,
for you, famous son of Abraham,
from noble, keen Esau, [lord] of ranks of hosts,
your delightful son of many victories."

"Do not doubt me, splendid elder,
father, son of Abraham;
to you, with keen love, I am Esau;
I tell you no lie."

"Though I put my hand in your hand —
I will not hide it — I am in your fair company;
I know you, it is you who reared me [?];
strongly it stirs my doubt."

"Perhaps it is Esau, of pure line;
perhaps it is not Jacob;
perhaps my lack of wisdom is plain;
perhaps it is I who am deceived."

"Brothers, consider your honour;
do nothing that is wrong;
though some misfortune should befall me,
nothing [?] would save you." [?]

"Alas, your sense is not sound,
Joseph, son of Rachel;
[?] foolish, without clear wisdom;
surely your dreams have deceived you."

"Lady, a blessing on your pure face;
do not move me with your foolish words;
as long as I live — bright victory —
I will not betray my master."

Then Joseph went in;
he was not disobedient or [?];
he knew nothing at all of the woman's design
until he was behind the doors.

He escaped from her and went out
naked from her hands;
the woman shouted at the top of her voice;
she had parted from true, pure modesty.

"A vision has appeared to me, a wondrous sound;
the fire of its [?] has burned me;
someone to explain it — a just sound —
I have not found among the minds of Egypt."

"'Give your mind to it without guile;
it is clear to every man of understanding;
the world will be full [?]
of the tales of this vision.'"

"You will have from me the pledge of God himself;
you will have my dignity clearly;
sun and moon together,
sea and land, dew and [?]."

"Famine with horror has driven us
from the north, from the lands of Canaan, [?],
from our fair country,
as the hosts of the men of the world are coming."

"The land of Ham has sheltered us from long ago;
our father was [descended] from the Chaldeans;
he was reared without [?], without horror;
he was son of Isaac son of Abraham."

The dear light stood still
at the command of God from holy heaven;
the men of the host of Canaan were all dead together;
it did not move from that one place.

The ninth to Gad, without doubt,
with valour, by the river to the south-east;
the tenth lot to Judah perforce;
the choicest portion to Reuben.

except that the youngest escaped him,
Jotham, [?] the heavy hosts;
it was a sad deed for [?]:
he slaughtered them upon one stone.

through the host of foxes, a bright tale,
for burning the vineyards;
through the hard jawbone,
by which a thousand of the great host perished;

that he should not take with him their cloaks,
their garments, their gold, their silver;
that he should lay all on the ground,
both woman and man, in red slaughter;

Ruth the mother of Obed, a clear saying;
Obed the father of Jesse;
Jesse, beyond scorn or loss,
was the true father of David.

He put them in dread [?] about the meeting [?];
he crushed them [?] without pride, without combat;
a bright cry [?] rose above every assembly;
a champion each day to their ruin.

“I will kill Goliath, a bright deed;
he shall be [?] for our friendship [?];
my might will be victorious over him;
by the will of my King I will go.”
“I will go,” said David without woe…

David, without guilt [?], won victory
from the holy, pure King of heaven;
not wretched the [?] before his face;
swiftly he went without a breastplate.
David went without a breastplate, a swift array…

“Do not go, if you will, bright youth,
to the son of Kish without protection;
he would be foolish unless he [?] a secret;
do not trust Saul.”

“O son of Kish, take hold of your deeds;
do not break [?] over your great boasting [?];
do not drown, through mighty fury,
the head of the [?] of Israel.”

“O son of Kish of good practice, bright deed,
do not pursue the son of Jesse;
[?] customary, bright destruction [?];
do not outrage your good daughter.

Michal waged a fierce quarrel
with Saul and with Ahinoam;
her fight was not restrained;
she was grieving, bitterly contentious.

“If it falls far, without grief,
take a step that settles your full welfare [?];
if not far, [?],
come quickly to the royal house.”

David went away, eager for grace [?],
without spear, without shield, without sword;
[?] rough, he [?] valour;
he left his weapons in the royal house.

Then a sharp sickness seized him;
in anxiety he was not very strong;
his mind was disturbed,
and every frenzy began.

He found, away from his father's people [?],
a cave in the lands of the wilderness;
for the needs of his body,
he went into it to relieve himself.

“What was promised you long ago
has been brought under your command;
long this victory [?];
in this hour it has come.

"and your sons will die, a just measure,
for your sin, for your transgression;
and a woeful, avenging, heavy slaughter
will fall upon the angry host of Israel;

They fought a long battle with a hundred great deeds;
bands of men pressed against warriors;
in the battle of Gilboa, with his host,
Saul was then killed.
Saul, the long-wandering slayer of hosts.

Ishbosheth son of Saul, without grief,
had taken the kingship of Israel;
he took Michal, though it grieved Paltiel,
from Paltiel son of Laish,

David, with perfect word, would not let them
be seen [?] by the great host;
for their recovery, for their growing again [?],
they stayed in Jericho.

when he had the number of the hosts counted
that were under his power, great in clans;
when he took the woman he desired;
when he killed her strong soldier.

"seven years of lasting poverty for him,
and famine with its hardship [?];
or three months of being driven before plunderers;
or a bout of sickness for three days."

for overthrowing [?] everyone against the law,
after abandoning [?] the cause of true right,
for the worship of God, with desires of valour [?],
for the worship of fierce idols;

for driving out strange foreigners [?],
with the lines of their kin in company [?];
for a bright deed, brighter than any form,
for ordering God's will [?].

The King who gave them many plants
to spread throughout the earth;
they all grow together;
though they differ, they are alike.

Wonderful is the King of great grace;
they cannot be told for their multitude;
more numerous are the plants of the slender wood
than the bright-pointed stars.

The King who saved Abel without disgrace
for the beloved honour of the great world,
for his choice, for love's sake,
through his humility, through his pure offering.

Darkness spread over every plain;
the dead of the earth rose up;
the elements of dear God were afraid
when the veil of the temple was torn.

Every element shook — a pure sound;
heaven and earth trembled;
the sea strove to pass its bounds;
the hearts of dark stones split apart.

For the clergy of the apostles without sin,
for the host of the perfect disciples,
for every saint with kingly grace,
forgive me my misdeeds.
